In this lesson, you will learn

4
00:00:07.474 --> 00:00:09.175
all about the steps you can follow

5
00:00:09.275 --> 00:00:12.479
to make your agent production ready using tools like MLflow

6
00:00:12.779 --> 00:00:15.749
and custom evaluation metrics. Let's get to it.

7
00:00:15.749 --> 00:00:18.151
In our first lab, we built and prepared

8
00:00:18.151 --> 00:00:20.353
the data layer with governance.

9
00:00:20.553 --> 00:00:22.355
Now, we're going to build the agents,

10
00:00:22.555 --> 00:00:25.658
evaluate them and deploy them in labs two and three.

11
00:00:25.925 --> 00:00:29.095
Just a little refresh for those who are newer to agents.

12
00:00:29.396 --> 00:00:33.900
The agent uses the LLM as its brain for reasoning through tools.

13
00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:37.704
So for example, I can give an agent access

14
00:00:37.704 --> 00:00:39.706
to a series of tools

15
00:00:39.906 --> 00:00:42.409
and I'm going to give it an LLM

16
00:00:42.409 --> 00:00:44.711
and normally a system prompt so that it can make decisions.

17
00:00:44.911 --> 00:00:48.415
So if I have a customer asking my agent,

18
00:00:48.615 --> 00:00:50.717
Can you help me return my last order?

19
00:00:50.917 --> 00:00:52.819
The way a normal customer support

20
00:00:52.819 --> 00:00:55.321
agent would work is they would say, "Okay,

21
00:00:55.321 --> 00:00:59.025
let me look up your account. Let me find that information.

22
00:00:59.325 --> 00:01:01.628
Let me look up our own return policy,

23
00:01:01.628 --> 00:01:04.998
make sure the item you want to return falls within that policy,

24
00:01:05.298 --> 00:01:08.601
and then go through the process to generate a shipping label.

25
00:01:08.802 --> 00:01:12.005
The agent should go through that same reasoning step.

26
00:01:12.305 --> 00:01:15.308
So a reasoning action or ReAct paradigm,

27
00:01:15.408 --> 00:01:17.010
call the tools necessary

28
00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:19.312
and ultimately resolve the issue.

29
00:01:19.712 --> 00:01:21.915
The tools can be documents,

30
00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:24.717
they can be vector databases, they could be functions,

31
00:01:24.717 --> 00:01:27.720
they could be APIs, they could be tables.

32
00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:30.723
You can give a lot of different tools to your agent

33
00:01:30.723 --> 00:01:33.726
and as long as the agent's able to understand

34
00:01:33.726 --> 00:01:35.695
the data that it's using,

35
00:01:35.695 --> 00:01:38.498
either through prompts

36
00:01:38.498 --> 00:01:41.201
or explicit actions on behalf of those tools,

37
00:01:41.201 --> 00:01:46.201
it should be able to then generate proper responses and retrieve data.

38
00:01:46.606 --> 00:01:50.410
For evaluating AI Agents, so after your Agent is built,

39
00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:52.612
you're going to want to evaluate the

40
00:01:52.612 --> 00:01:55.115
performance of the system before deploying into production.

41
00:01:55.415 --> 00:01:58.618
So the main way in which we evaluate are using Retrieval metrics

42
00:01:58.618 --> 00:02:03.223
such as RAG metrics, so whether or not a response is relevant,

43
00:02:03.623 --> 00:02:06.826
Response metrics, so if your agent is hallucinating,

44
00:02:06.826 --> 00:02:09.629
if it's saying something that is incorrect,

45
00:02:09.829 --> 00:02:11.831
if it's saying something that is unsafe.

46
00:02:12.031 --> 00:02:14.934
Also looking at the speed, latency, and cost.

47
00:02:15.034 --> 00:02:16.669
So the cost of building an agent

48
00:02:16.669 --> 00:02:18.338
and the cost of maintaining an agent.

49
00:02:18.638 --> 00:02:21.641
You also just need to make sure that it is fast enough

50
00:02:21.741 --> 00:02:24.177
that the user is going to be able

51
00:02:24.177 --> 00:02:26.613
to get a response in a timely manner.

52
00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:29.349
The three ways in which we generally run

53
00:02:29.349 --> 00:02:31.651
evals on your agent is through code-based evals.

54
00:02:31.951 --> 00:02:35.054
These are your JSON and regex expressions.

55
00:02:35.355 --> 00:02:36.956
Your LLM as a judge,

56
00:02:37.056 --> 00:02:42.028
so LLM labeled data via a prompt template or metrics like hallucinations.

57
00:02:42.529 --> 00:02:44.297
And then human in the loop evals.

58
00:02:44.297 --> 00:02:46.032
So this is your golden data set.

59
00:02:46.132 --> 00:02:48.301
This is having a subject matter

60
00:02:48.301 --> 00:02:50.837
expert either label data or provide you

61
00:02:51.237 --> 00:02:53.840
a set of inputs and outputs that you can use

62
00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:56.242
to train or fine tune your agent.

63
00:02:56.609 --> 00:02:58.811
I'm just going back to LLM-as-a-judge.

64
00:02:58.811 --> 00:03:01.814
This has become the most popular way to evaluate agents

65
00:03:01.814 --> 00:03:03.383
because a lot of agents do

66
00:03:03.383 --> 00:03:05.218
not have ground truths to draw on.

67
00:03:05.518 --> 00:03:08.421
But it's easy to make a secondary agent

68
00:03:08.421 --> 00:03:11.791
that can evaluate maybe the tone of the agent

69
00:03:12.091 --> 00:03:14.794
or very custom metrics that you want to build

70
00:03:14.794 --> 00:03:17.397
when building your agents and making sure they're performant

71
00:03:17.397 --> 00:03:19.399
on specific metrics for production.

72
00:03:19.799 --> 00:03:23.403
Remember, these evals, once you turn your model into

73
00:03:23.403 --> 00:03:25.605
a production deployed system,

74
00:03:26.172 --> 00:03:28.741
These evals will turn into monitoring capabilities.

75
00:03:28.741 --> 00:03:32.178
So you will now have thresholds on those initial evaluations,

76
00:03:32.178 --> 00:03:34.280
so you could begin monitoring them.

77
00:03:34.681 --> 00:03:36.583
Monitoring for key performance metrics

78
00:03:36.583 --> 00:03:40.086
in addition for monitoring things like cost and token count.

79
00:03:40.587 --> 00:03:42.355
When preparing for production,

80
00:03:42.655 --> 00:03:45.325
the ways to run your evals on your

81
00:03:45.325 --> 00:03:47.961
agent is through a lot of tracing capabilities.

82
00:03:48.361 --> 00:03:52.165
Tracing is most commonly used for OTel frameworks

83
00:03:52.165 --> 00:03:55.668
or open telemetry format. That's kind of the standard when logging

84
00:03:55.735 --> 00:03:59.939
this traces. MLflow tracing also use the open telemetry format

85
00:03:59.939 --> 00:04:02.575
to log all traces and then every step

86
00:04:02.575 --> 00:04:05.545
within a trace, which is also called a span.

87
00:04:05.745 --> 00:04:08.648
So you can see how long each step took,

88
00:04:09.048 --> 00:04:12.719
see the inputs, outputs, the attributes of the LLM,

89
00:04:13.019 --> 00:04:15.755
see the intermediate steps, see all the

90
00:04:15.755 --> 00:04:18.825
documents retrieved if you're using a RAG-based agent,

91
00:04:19.626 --> 00:04:22.528
and really start being able to troubleshoot

92
00:04:22.528 --> 00:04:24.731
and resolve problems with your agent in production.

93
00:04:25.298 --> 00:04:28.101
In this course when you build your agent,

94
00:04:28.101 --> 00:04:30.303
we're going to log the agent in MLflow.

95
00:04:30.303 --> 00:04:34.507
So logging saves your agent as a versioned model artifact

96
00:04:34.507 --> 00:04:36.509
in MLflow's model registry,

97
00:04:36.509 --> 00:04:39.212
and you can think of this as saving your agent

98
00:04:39.212 --> 00:04:43.216
in a format that Databricks can deploy and manage in production.

99
00:04:43.316 --> 00:04:48.254
So, it stores the agent code, the dependencies, and the full configuration.

100
00:04:48.755 --> 00:04:51.424
It creates a deployable model version with metadata.

101
00:04:51.824 --> 00:04:54.994
It enables model serving endpoints and API access.

102
00:04:55.328 --> 00:04:58.231
It tracks experiments, performance metrics, and lineage,

103
00:04:58.498 --> 00:05:00.733
and allows rollback to previous versions.

104
00:05:01.134 --> 00:05:03.936
When you're authoring AI agents in code,

105
00:05:03.936 --> 00:05:07.140
MLflow recommends using the ResponsesAgent interface

106
00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:09.042
for building enterprise agents.

107
00:05:09.042 --> 00:05:11.344
So no matter what agents you're using,

108
00:05:11.344 --> 00:05:14.147
whether you're using an agent from OpenAI,

109
00:05:14.147 --> 00:05:16.249
LangChain, Anthropic,

110
00:05:16.249 --> 00:05:19.452
if you're building an agent in any Python framework,

111
00:05:19.819 --> 00:05:23.923
format, this wraps the agent in the ResponsesAgent interface,

112
00:05:23.923 --> 00:05:26.025
so you have a unified interface.

113
00:05:26.025 --> 00:05:28.094
It's consistent input and output.

114
00:05:28.094 --> 00:05:31.297
There's no change to core agent logic or code.

115
00:05:31.297 --> 00:05:34.300
It's compatible with Databricks features and MLflow,

116
00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:38.004
